1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,020
I changed one line of code and had to ship four hundred

2
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:05,691
and one megabytes again.

3
00:00:05,691 --> 00:00:06,646
Same change,

4
00:00:06,646 --> 00:00:08,257
different image layout,

5
00:00:08,257 --> 00:00:09,809
and only one megabyte.

6
00:00:09,809 --> 00:00:12,912
And when I submitted three jobs to sixteen GPUs,

7
00:00:12,912 --> 00:00:16,134
ninety-five percent of the time the cluster froze.

8
00:00:16,484 --> 00:00:20,642
Both problems are mistakes beginners make with containers and Kubernetes.

9
00:00:20,642 --> 00:00:22,101
Both have simple fixes.

10
00:00:22,101 --> 00:00:23,721
By the end of this lesson,

11
00:00:23,721 --> 00:00:26,853
you will know them and be able to prove them yourself.

12
00:00:28,103 --> 00:00:31,535
Welcome to lesson three of the cloud for A I engineers course,

13
00:00:31,535 --> 00:00:32,835
on The Code Notebook.

14
00:00:33,185 --> 00:00:36,320
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

15
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:37,241
the description.

16
00:00:38,491 --> 00:00:41,699
In lesson two we chose chips and learned to pay for them.

17
00:00:41,699 --> 00:00:43,856
Now we need to run something on them.

18
00:00:43,856 --> 00:00:46,959
That means packing your model service into a container,

19
00:00:46,959 --> 00:00:49,167
and telling a cluster how to place it.

20
00:00:49,517 --> 00:00:51,383
This lesson has two halves.

21
00:00:51,383 --> 00:00:52,707
First, containers.

22
00:00:52,707 --> 00:00:53,911
Then Kubernetes,

23
00:00:53,911 --> 00:00:55,596
and the tools around it.

24
00:00:55,596 --> 00:00:58,425
Docker was not running on my build machine,

25
00:00:58,425 --> 00:01:00,772
so I label my experiments honestly:

26
00:01:00,772 --> 00:01:02,638
real bytes and real hashes,

27
00:01:02,638 --> 00:01:06,008
with a simulated registry and a simulated scheduler.

28
00:01:07,258 --> 00:01:08,555
What is a container?

29
00:01:08,555 --> 00:01:09,853
Think of a meal kit.

30
00:01:09,853 --> 00:01:12,447
The box has the recipe and every ingredient,

31
00:01:12,447 --> 00:01:14,987
so the dish tastes the same in any kitchen.

32
00:01:15,337 --> 00:01:17,023
A container holds your code,

33
00:01:17,023 --> 00:01:18,974
your libraries and your settings,

34
00:01:18,974 --> 00:01:20,028
packed together.

35
00:01:20,028 --> 00:01:21,925
It runs the same on your laptop,

36
00:01:21,925 --> 00:01:24,350
on a colleague's machine and in the cloud.

37
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:26,564
The packed version is called an image,

38
00:01:26,564 --> 00:01:28,936
and a running copy is called a container.

39
00:01:30,186 --> 00:01:31,704
AI adds one wrinkle.

40
00:01:31,704 --> 00:01:36,322
A container shares the operating system kernel of the machine it runs

41
00:01:36,322 --> 00:01:39,802
on. The GPU's driver is deeply tied to that kernel.

42
00:01:39,802 --> 00:01:42,712
So the driver cannot be packed in the box.

43
00:01:43,062 --> 00:01:44,368
The split is this.

44
00:01:44,368 --> 00:01:46,802
The host machine owns the GPU driver.

45
00:01:46,802 --> 00:01:50,067
Your image owns the CUDA libraries your code calls.

46
00:01:50,417 --> 00:01:53,340
And a piece of software called the N V I D I

47
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:55,653
A Container Toolkit is the bridge.

48
00:01:55,653 --> 00:01:58,028
At the moment the container starts,

49
00:01:58,028 --> 00:02:01,986
it connects the GPU and the host's driver files into the box.

50
00:02:03,236 --> 00:02:05,150
So what goes in your image?

51
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:07,065
The CUDA runtime libraries,

52
00:02:07,065 --> 00:02:08,980
such as cuBLAS and N C C L.

53
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:10,401
If you use PyTorch,

54
00:02:10,401 --> 00:02:12,933
its pip package already bundles them.

55
00:02:12,933 --> 00:02:13,984
The compiler,

56
00:02:13,984 --> 00:02:14,972
called nvcc,

57
00:02:14,972 --> 00:02:17,072
is only needed while building,

58
00:02:17,072 --> 00:02:18,369
never at runtime.

59
00:02:18,719 --> 00:02:22,074
That suggests a trick called a multi stage build.

60
00:02:22,074 --> 00:02:24,859
Build with the heavy tools in one stage.

61
00:02:24,859 --> 00:02:27,264
Copy only the result into a clean,

62
00:02:27,264 --> 00:02:30,303
small runtime image with no compiler inside.

63
00:02:31,553 --> 00:02:33,424
There is one rule to remember.

64
00:02:33,424 --> 00:02:36,837
The host driver must be new enough for the CUDA version in

65
00:02:36,837 --> 00:02:37,663
your image.

66
00:02:37,663 --> 00:02:41,901
The commonly quoted minimums are driver five twenty-five for CUDA twelve,

67
00:02:41,901 --> 00:02:43,993
and five eighty for CUDA thirteen.

68
00:02:44,343 --> 00:02:47,904
Check the official N V I D I A compatibility page for

69
00:02:47,904 --> 00:02:49,278
the exact numbers.

70
00:02:49,278 --> 00:02:52,965
If a container starts and says no CUDA device is found,

71
00:02:52,965 --> 00:02:56,151
the first suspect is an old driver on the host,

72
00:02:56,151 --> 00:02:57,276
not your code.

73
00:02:58,526 --> 00:02:59,701
A security note.

74
00:02:59,701 --> 00:03:01,228
In twenty twenty-five,

75
00:03:01,228 --> 00:03:06,164
a critical flaw nicknamed NVIDIAScape let a malicious image escape its container

76
00:03:06,164 --> 00:03:07,574
through the toolkit.

77
00:03:07,574 --> 00:03:11,569
It was fixed in toolkit version one point seventeen point eight.

78
00:03:11,919 --> 00:03:14,415
The lesson is that on a shared cluster,

79
00:03:14,415 --> 00:03:16,563
you run images you did not build.

80
00:03:16,563 --> 00:03:19,175
Rebuilding your own images does not help.

81
00:03:19,175 --> 00:03:20,801
You must patch the host.

82
00:03:20,801 --> 00:03:23,878
Keep the toolkit and the GPU operator up to date.

83
00:03:25,128 --> 00:03:26,564
Now to the experiment.

84
00:03:26,564 --> 00:03:28,443
An image is a stack of layers,

85
00:03:28,443 --> 00:03:29,327
like bricks.

86
00:03:29,327 --> 00:03:33,361
A layer's identity depends on its contents and on every layer beneath

87
00:03:33,361 --> 00:03:33,747
it.

88
00:03:34,097 --> 00:03:35,801
So if you change one brick,

89
00:03:35,801 --> 00:03:39,043
that brick and every brick above it must be made again,

90
00:03:39,043 --> 00:03:40,252
and shipped again.

91
00:03:40,252 --> 00:03:41,955
Here is a typical AI image.

92
00:03:41,955 --> 00:03:43,109
Operating system,

93
00:03:43,109 --> 00:03:44,153
CUDA libraries,

94
00:03:44,153 --> 00:03:45,252
Python packages,

95
00:03:45,252 --> 00:03:46,241
model weights,

96
00:03:46,241 --> 00:03:47,670
and finally your code.

97
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:50,735
Where you put the heavy weights in that stack turns out to

98
00:03:50,735 --> 00:03:51,479
matter a lot.

99
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:55,032
I built the layers from real files.

100
00:03:55,032 --> 00:03:57,689
Fifty megabytes for the operating system,

101
00:03:57,689 --> 00:03:59,165
one hundred for CUDA,

102
00:03:59,165 --> 00:04:00,936
sixty for Python packages,

103
00:04:00,936 --> 00:04:03,003
four hundred for model weights,

104
00:04:03,003 --> 00:04:04,006
one for code.

105
00:04:04,006 --> 00:04:05,600
I hashed them for real,

106
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:08,021
the way a registry identifies layers.

107
00:04:08,371 --> 00:04:11,222
Then I pushed the image to a pretend registry,

108
00:04:11,222 --> 00:04:12,875
changed one line of code,

109
00:04:12,875 --> 00:04:14,073
and pushed again.

110
00:04:14,073 --> 00:04:17,208
I counted the bytes that had to ship a second time.

111
00:04:17,208 --> 00:04:19,147
Docker itself was not running,

112
00:04:19,147 --> 00:04:21,200
so the registry is a simulation.

113
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:24,112
Layout A puts the code last,

114
00:04:24,112 --> 00:04:25,463
on top of the weights.

115
00:04:25,463 --> 00:04:27,594
Changing the code ships one megabyte.

116
00:04:27,944 --> 00:04:30,344
Layout B puts the code before the weights,

117
00:04:30,344 --> 00:04:32,379
so the weights layer sits above it.

118
00:04:32,379 --> 00:04:35,615
One line of code now ships four hundred and one megabytes.

119
00:04:35,965 --> 00:04:39,502
And layout C keeps the weights out of the image altogether.

120
00:04:39,502 --> 00:04:40,737
Also one megabyte.

121
00:04:40,737 --> 00:04:41,917
Same code change,

122
00:04:41,917 --> 00:04:43,938
a four hundred times difference.

123
00:04:45,188 --> 00:04:46,532
So the rules follow.

124
00:04:46,532 --> 00:04:48,437
Put what changes rarely first,

125
00:04:48,437 --> 00:04:50,230
and what changes often last.

126
00:04:50,230 --> 00:04:51,406
Operating system,

127
00:04:51,406 --> 00:04:52,191
then CUDA,

128
00:04:52,191 --> 00:04:53,591
then Python packages,

129
00:04:53,591 --> 00:04:54,656
then your code.

130
00:04:55,006 --> 00:04:57,229
Pin your base image by its digest,

131
00:04:57,229 --> 00:04:58,224
not by a tag,

132
00:04:58,224 --> 00:05:01,090
so a rebuild next month gives the same image.

133
00:05:01,090 --> 00:05:02,612
Commit your lock file.

134
00:05:02,612 --> 00:05:06,239
Use cache mounts for package downloads so builds are fast.

135
00:05:07,489 --> 00:05:10,723
Here is the whole idea as a sketch of a build file.

136
00:05:10,723 --> 00:05:14,427
The first stage is the heavy build stage with the compiler.

137
00:05:14,427 --> 00:05:17,367
Copy the lock file and install packages first.

138
00:05:17,367 --> 00:05:20,660
That layer stays cached until the lock file changes.

139
00:05:21,010 --> 00:05:23,123
Only then copy your source code.

140
00:05:23,123 --> 00:05:26,293
The final stage starts from a clean runtime image,

141
00:05:26,293 --> 00:05:28,348
copies the finished app across,

142
00:05:28,348 --> 00:05:30,168
and sets the start command.

143
00:05:30,168 --> 00:05:33,220
Verify the exact image tags before you use this,

144
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:34,629
because they change.

145
00:05:35,879 --> 00:05:37,592
Now the big question for AI.

146
00:05:37,592 --> 00:05:40,215
Do you bake the model weights into the image,

147
00:05:40,215 --> 00:05:41,767
or mount them at runtime?

148
00:05:42,117 --> 00:05:45,277
Baking is simple and gives you one tidy artifact.

149
00:05:45,277 --> 00:05:47,185
It is fine for small models,

150
00:05:47,185 --> 00:05:48,855
up to a gigabyte or two.

151
00:05:48,855 --> 00:05:50,822
For big models it is painful,

152
00:05:50,822 --> 00:05:54,697
because every new model version is a huge new layer and pulls

153
00:05:54,697 --> 00:05:55,651
become slow.

154
00:05:56,001 --> 00:05:59,575
Mounting keeps the app image small and lets you update the model

155
00:05:59,575 --> 00:06:01,256
without rebuilding anything.

156
00:06:02,506 --> 00:06:03,820
I measured that too.

157
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:04,532
Baked in,

158
00:06:04,532 --> 00:06:08,474
a new model version ships four hundred and one megabytes with layout

159
00:06:08,474 --> 00:06:10,554
A, and four hundred with layout B.

160
00:06:10,904 --> 00:06:13,522
Mounted, the app image does not change at all.

161
00:06:13,522 --> 00:06:16,350
The weights ship separately as their own artifact,

162
00:06:16,350 --> 00:06:17,868
and the app is untouched.

163
00:06:18,218 --> 00:06:20,056
So big weights get their own life.

164
00:06:20,056 --> 00:06:21,604
They change on one schedule,

165
00:06:21,604 --> 00:06:22,813
your code on another.

166
00:06:24,063 --> 00:06:26,032
How do you mount a big model?

167
00:06:26,032 --> 00:06:28,658
Kubernetes now supports an image volume,

168
00:06:28,658 --> 00:06:32,179
where the weights are their own artifact in a registry,

169
00:06:32,179 --> 00:06:34,268
mounted read only into the pod.

170
00:06:34,268 --> 00:06:38,505
That became generally available in Kubernetes one point thirty-six.

171
00:06:38,855 --> 00:06:42,361
Other options are a fast local disk cache on the machine,

172
00:06:42,361 --> 00:06:43,913
a shared network drive,

173
00:06:43,913 --> 00:06:46,614
or object storage through a storage driver.

174
00:06:46,614 --> 00:06:48,339
Pin the weights by digest,

175
00:06:48,339 --> 00:06:49,833
just like your images.

176
00:06:51,083 --> 00:06:52,181
Now Kubernetes.

177
00:06:52,181 --> 00:06:55,707
It is a system that runs containers across many machines.

178
00:06:55,707 --> 00:06:57,499
You describe what you want,

179
00:06:57,499 --> 00:06:59,060
and it makes it happen.

180
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:01,661
A cluster is the whole group of machines.

181
00:07:01,661 --> 00:07:03,164
A node is one machine.

182
00:07:03,514 --> 00:07:06,436
A pod is one or more containers running together.

183
00:07:06,436 --> 00:07:08,365
The scheduler is the head chef.

184
00:07:08,365 --> 00:07:09,854
It looks at each order,

185
00:07:09,854 --> 00:07:10,405
a pod,

186
00:07:10,405 --> 00:07:12,720
and decides which node should cook it.

187
00:07:13,970 --> 00:07:16,636
How does Kubernetes know about GPUs?

188
00:07:16,636 --> 00:07:19,569
A small helper program on each GPU node,

189
00:07:19,569 --> 00:07:21,369
called a device plugin,

190
00:07:21,369 --> 00:07:22,635
announces them.

191
00:07:22,635 --> 00:07:26,168
The announcement is a resource called N V I D I A

192
00:07:26,168 --> 00:07:27,634
dot com slash gpu.

193
00:07:27,984 --> 00:07:30,218
A pod asks for whole numbers.

194
00:07:30,218 --> 00:07:31,030
One GPU,

195
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:31,910
two GPUs,

196
00:07:31,910 --> 00:07:32,925
never half.

197
00:07:32,925 --> 00:07:34,076
And for GPUs,

198
00:07:34,076 --> 00:07:37,054
the request and the limit must be equal.

199
00:07:37,054 --> 00:07:38,678
That is the classic,

200
00:07:38,678 --> 00:07:41,453
safest way to give a pod a whole GPU.

201
00:07:42,703 --> 00:07:44,689
GPU machines are expensive,

202
00:07:44,689 --> 00:07:46,932
so keep ordinary pods off them.

203
00:07:46,932 --> 00:07:49,046
Put a taint on the GPU nodes.

204
00:07:49,046 --> 00:07:51,289
A taint is a do not enter sign,

205
00:07:51,289 --> 00:07:55,646
and only pods that carry the matching toleration may land there.

206
00:07:55,996 --> 00:07:57,304
Also use labels.

207
00:07:57,304 --> 00:08:01,818
A tool called GPU Feature Discovery labels each node with its GPU

208
00:08:01,818 --> 00:08:03,191
model and memory,

209
00:08:03,191 --> 00:08:06,789
so a pod can ask for an H one hundred specifically.

210
00:08:08,039 --> 00:08:10,692
Setting up each node by hand is tedious.

211
00:08:10,692 --> 00:08:14,972
NVIDIA's GPU Operator installs the whole node stack as one package.

212
00:08:14,972 --> 00:08:15,876
The driver,

213
00:08:15,876 --> 00:08:17,444
the container toolkit,

214
00:08:17,444 --> 00:08:18,770
the device plugin,

215
00:08:18,770 --> 00:08:20,157
the labelling tool,

216
00:08:20,157 --> 00:08:21,302
and monitoring.

217
00:08:21,652 --> 00:08:24,237
The version names follow year and month.

218
00:08:24,237 --> 00:08:28,936
The latest in October twenty twenty-six is twenty-six point seven point one.

219
00:08:28,936 --> 00:08:31,638
If your cloud image already ships drivers,

220
00:08:31,638 --> 00:08:34,399
you turn off the operator's driver install.

221
00:08:35,649 --> 00:08:38,712
A whole GPU is wasteful for a small model.

222
00:08:38,712 --> 00:08:41,242
There are three ways to share one.

223
00:08:41,242 --> 00:08:43,705
MIG cuts a GPU into walled rooms,

224
00:08:43,705 --> 00:08:46,435
each with its own memory and compute.

225
00:08:46,785 --> 00:08:49,354
MPS gives clients desks in the same room.

226
00:08:49,354 --> 00:08:51,124
They get a share of memory,

227
00:08:51,124 --> 00:08:52,094
but no walls.

228
00:08:52,444 --> 00:08:55,310
Time slicing just takes turns on one chair.

229
00:08:55,310 --> 00:08:57,871
No memory isolation and no fair share.

230
00:08:57,871 --> 00:09:00,614
Only walls stop a crash in the next room.

231
00:09:01,864 --> 00:09:03,125
So which to pick?

232
00:09:03,125 --> 00:09:06,485
MIG for several teams or customers sharing hardware,

233
00:09:06,485 --> 00:09:08,225
because it isolates them.

234
00:09:08,225 --> 00:09:10,325
It works only on certain chips,

235
00:09:10,325 --> 00:09:11,465
in fixed sizes.

236
00:09:11,815 --> 00:09:12,906
MPS for small,

237
00:09:12,906 --> 00:09:14,784
cooperating inference jobs.

238
00:09:14,784 --> 00:09:18,056
Time slicing mostly for notebooks and development.

239
00:09:18,056 --> 00:09:20,601
If one tenant must never hurt another,

240
00:09:20,601 --> 00:09:22,237
only the walls will do.

241
00:09:23,487 --> 00:09:26,654
Two numbers make the sharing options concrete.

242
00:09:26,654 --> 00:09:28,048
With time slicing,

243
00:09:28,048 --> 00:09:32,608
a machine with eight GPUs set to ten replicas advertises eighty GPUs

244
00:09:32,608 --> 00:09:33,749
to Kubernetes,

245
00:09:33,749 --> 00:09:37,359
but they are still eight physical chips taking turns.

246
00:09:37,709 --> 00:09:38,499
With MPS,

247
00:09:38,499 --> 00:09:42,446
each client gets the memory divided by the number of clients.

248
00:09:42,446 --> 00:09:46,636
Eighty gigabytes shared by four clients is twenty gigabytes each.

249
00:09:46,636 --> 00:09:48,033
Sharing is a trade,

250
00:09:48,033 --> 00:09:49,369
not free capacity.

251
00:09:50,619 --> 00:09:53,240
There is a newer way to ask for devices,

252
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:55,563
called dynamic resource allocation.

253
00:09:55,563 --> 00:09:57,945
Instead of counting opaque integers,

254
00:09:57,945 --> 00:09:59,375
a pod makes a claim,

255
00:09:59,375 --> 00:10:01,221
a bit like a storage claim,

256
00:10:01,221 --> 00:10:03,664
and a driver offers matching devices.

257
00:10:03,664 --> 00:10:08,131
It has been generally available since Kubernetes one point thirty-four.

258
00:10:08,481 --> 00:10:09,449
In practice,

259
00:10:09,449 --> 00:10:13,380
production use today is mostly for the multi node link on the

260
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:14,469
biggest racks.

261
00:10:14,469 --> 00:10:16,949
For ordinary whole GPU training pods,

262
00:10:16,949 --> 00:10:19,792
the device plugin is still the safe choice.

263
00:10:19,792 --> 00:10:22,696
I mention DRA so the word is not new to you.

264
00:10:23,946 --> 00:10:25,824
Now the second horror story.

265
00:10:25,824 --> 00:10:29,228
Distributed training needs all of its workers running,

266
00:10:29,228 --> 00:10:30,930
or nobody makes progress.

267
00:10:30,930 --> 00:10:34,217
But the default scheduler places pods one at a time.

268
00:10:34,567 --> 00:10:37,420
So job A grabs three of its four machines.

269
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:39,094
Job B grabs the fourth.

270
00:10:39,094 --> 00:10:40,210
Both sit idle,

271
00:10:40,210 --> 00:10:41,885
holding expensive GPUs,

272
00:10:41,885 --> 00:10:44,737
waiting for pods that can never be placed.

273
00:10:44,737 --> 00:10:47,280
This is a partial placement deadlock.

274
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:49,202
The fix is gang scheduling:

275
00:10:49,202 --> 00:10:51,062
place all of a job's pods,

276
00:10:51,062 --> 00:10:51,806
or none.

277
00:10:53,056 --> 00:10:54,279
I simulated it.

278
00:10:54,279 --> 00:10:56,209
A cluster of sixteen GPUs.

279
00:10:56,209 --> 00:10:58,139
Each job needs eight pods,

280
00:10:58,139 --> 00:10:59,233
one GPU each,

281
00:10:59,233 --> 00:11:00,262
all at once.

282
00:11:00,262 --> 00:11:02,900
I shuffled the order the pods arrive,

283
00:11:02,900 --> 00:11:04,380
ten thousand times.

284
00:11:04,730 --> 00:11:07,892
The default scheduler places pods one by one.

285
00:11:07,892 --> 00:11:12,021
The gang scheduler places a job only when all eight GPUs are

286
00:11:12,021 --> 00:11:16,022
free. I counted how often the cluster ended up deadlocked.

287
00:11:16,022 --> 00:11:18,409
This is a simulation of the idea,

288
00:11:18,409 --> 00:11:20,926
not of a real Kubernetes scheduler.

289
00:11:22,176 --> 00:11:23,110
Two jobs fit,

290
00:11:23,110 --> 00:11:24,540
so no deadlock at all.

291
00:11:24,540 --> 00:11:25,749
Submit three jobs,

292
00:11:25,749 --> 00:11:30,092
and the default scheduler deadlocked ninety-four point eight percent of the

293
00:11:30,092 --> 00:11:30,587
time.

294
00:11:30,937 --> 00:11:31,820
Four jobs,

295
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:33,144
always. Six jobs,

296
00:11:33,144 --> 00:11:33,837
always.

297
00:11:34,187 --> 00:11:36,746
The gang scheduler never deadlocked once.

298
00:11:36,746 --> 00:11:38,167
Jobs wait their turn,

299
00:11:38,167 --> 00:11:40,385
and every job that starts finishes.

300
00:11:41,635 --> 00:11:44,091
In real clusters you use a queue tool.

301
00:11:44,091 --> 00:11:46,372
Kueue decides when a job may start,

302
00:11:46,372 --> 00:11:48,594
and enforces quotas between teams.

303
00:11:48,594 --> 00:11:51,635
The scheduler still decides where each pod goes.

304
00:11:51,985 --> 00:11:56,154
For the job itself you use helpers like JobSet or Ray.

305
00:11:56,154 --> 00:12:00,969
Other schedulers such as Volcano or KAI also do gang placement.

306
00:12:01,319 --> 00:12:04,490
Kubernetes is adding native gang scheduling too,

307
00:12:04,490 --> 00:12:08,514
but in version one point thirty-seven it is still beta and off

308
00:12:08,514 --> 00:12:09,428
by default.

309
00:12:09,428 --> 00:12:12,233
Do not build production on alpha features,

310
00:12:12,233 --> 00:12:13,940
because the APIs change.

311
00:12:15,190 --> 00:12:17,359
Which orchestrator for which job?

312
00:12:17,359 --> 00:12:20,173
Ray suits Python teams with mixed pipelines.

313
00:12:20,173 --> 00:12:20,993
Data prep,

314
00:12:20,993 --> 00:12:23,865
training, reinforcement learning and serving,

315
00:12:23,865 --> 00:12:25,624
all in one Python program.

316
00:12:25,974 --> 00:12:30,483
Kubeflow Trainer runs training jobs on Kubernetes the standard way.

317
00:12:30,483 --> 00:12:34,484
And KServe has grown into a serving layer for large models.

318
00:12:35,734 --> 00:12:39,004
And there is an older tool that big labs still love,

319
00:12:39,004 --> 00:12:39,997
called Slurm.

320
00:12:39,997 --> 00:12:43,092
It is a batch scheduler built for supercomputers.

321
00:12:43,092 --> 00:12:45,369
Huge pretraining runs often use it,

322
00:12:45,369 --> 00:12:49,515
and in late twenty twenty-five NVIDIA bought the company behind it.

323
00:12:49,865 --> 00:12:52,044
You do not have to pick one world.

324
00:12:52,044 --> 00:12:54,510
Hybrids run Slurm on top of Kubernetes.

325
00:12:54,510 --> 00:12:56,689
If you are fine tuning or serving,

326
00:12:56,689 --> 00:12:58,926
you will probably never need Slurm.

327
00:12:58,926 --> 00:13:00,933
If you join a pretraining team,

328
00:13:00,933 --> 00:13:01,678
you will.

329
00:13:02,928 --> 00:13:04,037
At large scale,

330
00:13:04,037 --> 00:13:05,087
machines fail.

331
00:13:05,087 --> 00:13:06,020
Plan for it.

332
00:13:06,020 --> 00:13:08,004
Run health checks on the GPUs,

333
00:13:08,004 --> 00:13:11,271
so a sick machine is removed before it spoils a job.

334
00:13:11,271 --> 00:13:14,597
Restart the whole gang together when one worker dies.

335
00:13:14,947 --> 00:13:16,396
And save checkpoints,

336
00:13:16,396 --> 00:13:18,310
exactly like our spot lesson.

337
00:13:18,310 --> 00:13:21,151
Containers and Kubernetes make restarts easy,

338
00:13:21,151 --> 00:13:23,528
but only checkpoints make them cheap.

339
00:13:24,778 --> 00:13:27,578
One small gotcha that costs people hours.

340
00:13:27,578 --> 00:13:31,186
The data loading workers in PyTorch use shared memory.

341
00:13:31,186 --> 00:13:32,680
The default is tiny.

342
00:13:32,680 --> 00:13:35,604
Give the pod a bigger shared memory volume,

343
00:13:35,604 --> 00:13:38,155
sixteen gigabytes is a common choice,

344
00:13:38,155 --> 00:13:41,266
or training will crash with a confusing error.

345
00:13:41,616 --> 00:13:44,497
It is a good example of what AI containers need.

346
00:13:44,497 --> 00:13:46,657
The defaults are made for web apps,

347
00:13:46,657 --> 00:13:47,821
not for training.

348
00:13:49,071 --> 00:13:50,602
For ShopNest Copilot,

349
00:13:50,602 --> 00:13:52,012
the plan is simple.

350
00:13:52,012 --> 00:13:54,585
One small image with the serving code,

351
00:13:54,585 --> 00:13:56,055
built in two stages,

352
00:13:56,055 --> 00:13:57,648
code on the top layer.

353
00:13:57,648 --> 00:14:00,834
The model weights mounted as their own artifact,

354
00:14:00,834 --> 00:14:02,120
pinned by digest.

355
00:14:02,470 --> 00:14:04,736
The pod asks for one whole GPU,

356
00:14:04,736 --> 00:14:06,548
tolerates the GPU taint,

357
00:14:06,548 --> 00:14:08,360
and runs behind a queue.

358
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:12,891
The nightly evaluation job becomes a gang job on the spot machines

359
00:14:12,891 --> 00:14:14,186
from lesson two.

360
00:14:15,436 --> 00:14:17,275
Four words to carry forward.

361
00:14:17,275 --> 00:14:19,172
Layer: one brick of an image.

362
00:14:19,172 --> 00:14:22,564
Mount: attach files at runtime instead of packing them.

363
00:14:22,914 --> 00:14:26,100
Taint: a sign that keeps the wrong pods off a machine.

364
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:27,418
And gang scheduling:

365
00:14:27,418 --> 00:14:29,561
place all of a job's pods together,

366
00:14:29,561 --> 00:14:30,220
or none.

367
00:14:31,470 --> 00:14:32,657
Interview corner.

368
00:14:32,657 --> 00:14:35,484
Asked how to speed up image builds and pushes,

369
00:14:35,484 --> 00:14:38,649
say: put rarely changing layers first and code last,

370
00:14:38,649 --> 00:14:40,176
use multi stage builds,

371
00:14:40,176 --> 00:14:42,889
and keep big model weights out of the image.

372
00:14:42,889 --> 00:14:46,676
In my experiment that turned a four hundred and one megabyte re

373
00:14:46,676 --> 00:14:47,694
push into one.

374
00:14:48,044 --> 00:14:51,851
Asked why distributed training jobs need gang scheduling,

375
00:14:51,851 --> 00:14:54,286
say: they need all workers at once,

376
00:14:54,286 --> 00:14:57,032
so placing pods one by one can deadlock.

377
00:14:57,032 --> 00:14:58,343
In my simulation,

378
00:14:58,343 --> 00:15:02,962
three jobs on sixteen GPUs deadlocked ninety-five percent of the time.

379
00:15:04,212 --> 00:15:04,967
Your turn.

380
00:15:04,967 --> 00:15:07,447
The zip for this lesson has three scripts.

381
00:15:07,447 --> 00:15:09,497
The first measures layer shipping.

382
00:15:09,497 --> 00:15:11,492
Change the layer sizes and order,

383
00:15:11,492 --> 00:15:13,487
and see what a code change costs.

384
00:15:13,837 --> 00:15:16,315
The second simulates gang scheduling.

385
00:15:16,315 --> 00:15:19,035
Change the cluster size and the job size.

386
00:15:19,035 --> 00:15:22,118
The third checks driver and CUDA compatibility.

387
00:15:22,118 --> 00:15:25,744
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

388
00:15:26,994 --> 00:15:29,077
Recap. The host owns the driver,

389
00:15:29,077 --> 00:15:31,275
the image owns the CUDA libraries,

390
00:15:31,275 --> 00:15:33,242
and the toolkit connects them.

391
00:15:33,242 --> 00:15:35,613
Layer order decides what you re ship,

392
00:15:35,613 --> 00:15:38,217
and big weights belong outside the image.

393
00:15:38,217 --> 00:15:41,977
Layout B shipped four hundred and one megabytes for one line.

394
00:15:42,327 --> 00:15:44,405
Kubernetes hands out whole GPUs,

395
00:15:44,405 --> 00:15:45,791
taints protect them,

396
00:15:45,791 --> 00:15:48,042
and gang scheduling stops deadlock.

397
00:15:48,042 --> 00:15:48,966
